    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d270">

                <pb facs="0410" n="320" sameAs="frus:frus1920v02/pg320"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">862t.01/40: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Observer on the
                        Rhineland High Commission</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Noyes</hi>)<anchor
                        n="33" xml:id="d270-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-04-09T13:00:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">April 9, 1920—1
                                p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>Your April 7. In Department’s opinion action of British authorities toward Red
                    troops taking refuge in British zone is correct, Department appreciates urgent
                    need of decision in matter in view of food situation in Ruhr district and hopes
                    that negotiations may be concluded promptly with German Government for
                    repatriation, particularly of workmen, under whatever guarantees may be
                    necessary.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Colby</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d270-f1" n="33" type="source" xml:id="d270-f1-content">
                <p>Via the Embassy in France as no. 727.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
